# Commands: sdd-explain

Purpose:
  Explainability-on-demand command. When a human requests an explanation
  of a completed task, the AI produces a structured explanation covering
  WHY the approach was chosen, WHAT changed, alternatives rejected,
  references, and risks. This supports the "AI + Human = simple"
  principle — humans get clarity without parsing 100 execution events.

Owns:
  /sdd-explain command definition
  Explanation template
  Explainability output format

Command:

  name: /sdd-explain
  alias: @explain
  description:
    Explain a completed task on demand. Provides WHY, WHAT changed,
    approach rationale, alternatives rejected, references, and risks.

  Usage:
    /sdd-explain TASK-<ID>
    @explain TASK-<ID>

  Options:
    --verbose    Include full execution trace
    --short      One-line summary only (default)

  Mode: READ_ONLY

ExplainabilityTemplate:

  /sdd-explain TASK-<ID>

  WHY:
    <business or technical reason this task was necessary>

  WHAT CHANGED:
    <concise summary of changes made>
    <files/layers affected>

  WHY THIS APPROACH:
    <rationale for the chosen approach>
    <how it satisfies constraints and knowledge)

  ALTERNATIVES REJECTED:
    <approach A>: <why it was not chosen>
    <approach B>: <why it was not chosen>

  REFERENCES:
    @decision.* <reason>
    @skill.* <reason>
    @concept.* <reason>
    @pattern.* <reason>

  RISKS:
    <risk 1>: <mitigation>
    <risk 2>: <mitigation>

  Example:
    /sdd-explain TASK-1023

    WHY:
      Refund operation lacked idempotency protection, causing duplicate
      payments when payment providers retried requests.

    WHAT CHANGED:
      - PaymentService: added idempotency key check before processing refund
      - RefundRepository: persisted idempotency key in transaction
      - Refund API: returns 409 Conflict on duplicate key
      - Outbox: transactional outbox for provider event publishing

    WHY THIS APPROACH:
      Idempotency key + transaction + outbox is the established pattern
      for payment reliability (@skill.payment.idempotency). The existing
      @decision.outbox-for-provider-events confirms the outbox approach
      for provider events.

    ALTERNATIVES REJECTED:
      Direct provider publish: caused deadlock under concurrent retries
        (observed in TASK-1018)
      In-memory dedup: not durable across restarts, violates
        @constraint.payment.durability

    REFERENCES:
      @skill.payment.idempotency — established pattern for payment operations
      @decision.outbox-for-provider-events — prior decision for event publishing
      @pattern.transactional-outbox — pattern for reliable event delivery
      @concept.idempotency — canonical concept definition

    RISKS:
      Idempotency key collision under high volume: mitigated by UUID v4
        + DB unique constraint
      Outbox table growth: mitigated by @decision.outbox-retention policy
      Breaking API change reverted: reverted during review, no impact

DefaultResponse:

  definition: when no --verbose flag, output is concise
  format:
    WHY: <one line>
    WHAT: <one line>
    HOW: <one line>
    RISK: <one line if any>

ExplainabilityRules:

  [EX1] /sdd-explain MUST ONLY run on COMPLETED tasks (or ESCALATED
        with human summary).
  [EX2] Explanation MUST reference @knowledge and @decision artifacts
        used during the task.
  [EX3] Alternatives rejected MUST include a rationale — "not chosen"
        alone is insufficient.
  [EX4] Risks MUST include a mitigation — "risk exists" alone is
        insufficient.
  [EX5] The default response is short; --verbose includes full trace.
  [EX6] Explainability is built from execution summary
        (bax EXECUTION-CONTRACT.sdd -> ExecutionSummary), not from
        raw runtime events.
  [EX7] If no NEW_DECISION was created by the task, the command MUST
        still cite the relevant existing decisions consulted.
  [EX8] Explainability supports the human-in-the-loop model — it does
        not replace human judgment, it informs it.

